Module = reusable code

A module is a collection of .tf files that tells Terraform how to create resources.

modules/
└── ec2/
    ├── main.tf
    ├── variables.tf
    └── outputs.tf

Workspace = separate Terraform state
A workspace lets you use the same Terraform configuration with a different state.
For example:
workspace: dev
    ↓
state for dev resources

workspace: prod
    ↓
state for prod resources


----------------
1. Folder structure
terraform-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── dev.tfvars
└── prod.tfvars
2. main.tf

This is the resource:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  tags = {
    Name = var.environment
  }
}

Here:

var.instance_type
        ↑
        value will come from .tfvars

var.environment
        ↑
        value will come from .tfvars
3. variables.tf

Here we declare the variables:

variable "ami" {
  type = string
}

variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}

variable "environment" {
  type = string
}

This does not give the values yet.

It only says:

I need:
  ami
  instance_type
  environment
4. dev.tfvars

Now we give the Dev values here:

ami           = "ami-123456"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
environment   = "dev"

So:

dev.tfvars
   │
   ├── ami           → ami-123456
   ├── instance_type → t2.micro
   └── environment   → dev
5. prod.tfvars

Now we give different Prod values:

ami           = "ami-123456"
instance_type = "t3.micro"
environment   = "prod"

So:

prod.tfvars
   │
   ├── ami           → ami-123456
   ├── instance_type → t3.micro
   └── environment   → prod
6. Create the Dev workspace

Run:

terraform workspace new dev

Now select Dev:

terraform workspace select dev

Then apply using dev.tfvars:

terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"

Terraform gets:

dev workspace
     ↓
dev.tfvars
     ↓
instance_type = t2.micro
environment   = dev
     ↓
main.tf
     ↓
EC2 t2.micro
Name = dev
7. Create the Prod workspace

First:

terraform workspace new prod

Then:

terraform workspace select prod

Apply using:

terraform apply -var-file="prod.tfvars"

Terraform gets:

prod workspace
     ↓
prod.tfvars
     ↓
instance_type = t3.micro
environment   = prod
     ↓
main.tf
     ↓
EC2 t3.micro
Name = prod
Final picture
                    main.tf
                       │
              ┌────────┴────────┐
              │                 │
          DEV WORKSPACE     PROD WORKSPACE
              │                 │
         dev.tfvars        prod.tfvars
              │                 │
         t2.micro           t3.micro
              │                 │
           dev EC2           prod EC2
              │                 │
          dev state         prod state
The important point

Workspace itself does not contain t2.micro or t3.micro.

We give those values through the variable file:

dev workspace  + dev.tfvars  → t2.micro
prod workspace + prod.tfvars → t3.micro

Then main.tf uses:

instance_type = var.instance_type

So the value travels:

dev.tfvars
   ↓
variable
   ↓
var.instance_type
   ↓
main.tf
   ↓
EC2

That's how the workspace + variable values work together.
