Let's combine Module + Workspace in one complete example. I’ll keep it simple and show exactly where each value goes.

Example

We want:

DEV       → t2.micro EC2
PROD      → t3.micro EC2

We want to use:

One EC2 module
Two workspaces: dev, prod
Different values for each workspace



1. Complete structure
terraform-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
│
├── dev.tfvars
├── prod.tfvars
│
└── modules/
    │
    └── ec2/
        ├── main.tf
        ├── variables.tf
        └── outputs.tf

There is only one EC2 module.
We reuse that same module for both Dev and Prod.


3. Root main.tf

Now we call the module:

module "ec2" {
  source = "./modules/ec2"

  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type
  environment   = var.environment
}

This is important.

The root says:

Root
 ↓
Call EC2 module
 ↓
Give it these values

4. Root variables.tf
variable "ami" {
  type = string
}

variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}

variable "environment" {
  type = string
}

Again, these only declare the variables.
They don't contain the actual values.

7. Root outputs.tf
output "instance_id" {
  value = module.ec2.instance_id
}

This displays the EC2 ID after apply.


5. Dev values

Create:  dev.tfvars
Put:
ami           = "ami-123456"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
environment   = "dev"

Now Dev has:
ami           → ami-123456
instance_type → t2.micro
environment   → dev


6. Prod values
Create:  prod.tfvars
Put:
ami           = "ami-123456"
instance_type = "t3.micro"
environment   = "prod"

Now Prod has:
ami           → ami-123456
instance_type → t3.micro
environment   → prod

2. Module

The module is:

modules/ec2/
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
└── outputs.tf
modules/ec2/variables.tf
variable "ami" {
  type = string
}

variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}

variable "environment" {
  type = string
}

This says:

"My EC2 module needs these 3 values."

It does not give the values yet.

modules/ec2/main.tf
resource "aws_instance" "this" {
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  tags = {
    Name = var.environment
  }
}

This says:
"Create an EC2 using the values I receive."
Notice:
instance_type = var.instance_type

The module does not know whether it will be:
t2.micro
or:
t3.micro

It will receive that value from the root.

modules/ec2/outputs.tf
output "instance_id" {
  value = aws_instance.this.id
}

This sends the EC2 ID out of the module.



8. Now create the Dev workspace
Run: terraform workspace new dev

Terraform creates the dev workspace.
Think:

Workspace
   ↓
dev

Then:  terraform workspace select dev
Now Terraform is working with the Dev state.

9. Apply Dev

Run:

terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"

Now watch the value flow:

dev.tfvars
     │
     │ instance_type = "t2.micro"
     ↓
root variable
     │
     │ var.instance_type
     ↓
root main.tf
     │
     │ instance_type = var.instance_type
     ↓
EC2 module
     │
     ↓
module variable
     │
     │ var.instance_type
     ↓
modules/ec2/main.tf
     │
     ↓
aws_instance
     │
     ↓
t2.micro EC2

So Dev creates:
EC2
t2.micro
Name = dev


10. Now create Prod workspace
Run:  terraform workspace new prod

Now you have:
dev
prod

Select Prod:  terraform workspace select prod
Now Terraform is working with the Prod state.


11. Apply Prod

Run:

terraform apply -var-file="prod.tfvars"

Value flow:

prod.tfvars
     │
     │ instance_type = "t3.micro"
     ↓
root variable
     ↓
root main.tf
     ↓
EC2 module
     ↓
var.instance_type
     ↓
aws_instance
     ↓
t3.micro EC2

So Prod creates:

EC2
t3.micro
Name = prod





12. What does Workspace actually do?

This is the most important part.

You have one code:

main.tf
modules/ec2/

But you have two workspaces:

             SAME CODE
                │
       ┌────────┴────────┐
       ↓                 ↓
      DEV               PROD
   workspace          workspace
       ↓                 ↓
   Dev state          Prod state
       ↓                 ↓
  Dev EC2            Prod EC2

The workspace does not create a second copy of your .tf files.

It gives Terraform a separate state.
--------------------------
If you're asking about local in Terraform, it means a local value — a value we define inside Terraform and reuse within that configuration.

For example:
locals {
  server_name = "${var.environment}-web-server"
}

Then use it:
resource "aws_instance" "this" {
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  tags = {
    Name = local.server_name
  }
}
Difference
variable
   ↓
Value comes FROM outside the module/configuration

local
   ↓
Value is CREATED/COMBINED INSIDE the configuration

For example:

variable "environment" {
  type = string
}

locals {
  server_name = "${var.environment}-web-server"
}

If:
environment = "dev"

then:
local.server_name
       ↓
dev-web-server

So:
Name = local.server_name

creates the AWS Name tag:  dev-web-server

var.xxx = input value
local.xxx = calculated/reusable value inside Terraform


the important part. Workspace mainly affects the state file.

Suppose your project has:

terraform-project/
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── dev.tfvars
├── prod.tfvars
└── modules/
    └── ec2/

You use the same code.

Without workspace

Normally you have:

terraform.tfstate

Terraform stores information about the resources it manages there.

For example:

terraform.tfstate
    ↓
EC2 ID = i-111111
With workspaces

You create:

terraform workspace new dev
terraform workspace new prod

Now Terraform keeps separate state for each workspace.

Conceptually:

DEV workspace
    ↓
Dev state
    ↓
EC2 = i-111111


PROD workspace
    ↓
Prod state
    ↓
EC2 = i-222222

With the default local backend, the state is typically organized like:

terraform.tfstate              ← default workspace

terraform.tfstate.d/
└── dev/
    └── terraform.tfstate

└── prod/
    └── terraform.tfstate

So the important point is:

SAME .tf CODE
       │
       ├── dev workspace → dev state → Dev EC2
       │
       └── prod workspace → prod state → Prod EC2
Why separate state?

Because Terraform needs to know which resources belong to which environment.

If you're in dev:

terraform workspace select dev
terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"

Terraform works with the Dev state.

If you're in prod:

terraform workspace select prod
terraform apply -var-file="prod.tfvars"

Terraform works with the Prod state.

So:
Workspace = separate state for the same Terraform code

------------------------------------


After creating dev and prod workspaces, you can have:

terraform-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── dev.tfvars
├── prod.tfvars
│
├── terraform.tfstate
│
└── terraform.tfstate.d/
    │
    ├── dev/
    │   └── terraform.tfstate
    │
    └── prod/
        └── terraform.tfstate


What each one means
terraform.tfstate
        ↓
default workspace state

And:
terraform.tfstate.d/
    ├── dev/
    │   └── terraform.tfstate
    │          ↓
    │       Dev resources
    │
    └── prod/
        └── terraform.tfstate
               ↓
            Prod resources

So when you run:
terraform workspace select dev
terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"

Terraform uses the Dev workspace state.

When you run:
terraform workspace select prod
terraform apply -var-file="prod.tfvars"

Terraform uses the Prod workspace state.

One important point
terraform.tfstate.d is the local backend's workspace storage layout. If you use a remote backend such as S3,
the state is stored remotely instead, with separate state paths/keys for workspaces rather than necessarily appearing in this local folder structure.

-----------------------------------
1. Complete project structure
terraform-project/
│
├── backend.tf
├── providers.tf
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
│
├── dev.tfvars
├── prod.tfvars
│
└── modules/
    │
    └── ec2/
        ├── main.tf
        ├── variables.tf
        └── outputs.tf

There is no need to manually create terraform.tfstate.

Terraform will manage the state in S3.


If you mean how we configure the state files in S3 for workspaces, here's the simple setup.
1. Create an S3 bucket
For example:   my-terraform-state-bucket
This bucket stores Terraform state remotely.

2. Configure the backend
In your root project, create backend.tf:

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
    key    = "terraform.tfstate"
    region = "ap-south-1"
  }
}

Now Terraform knows:
Terraform
   ↓
S3 backend
   ↓
my-terraform-state-bucket

3. Initialize
Run:  terraform init
Terraform configures the S3 backend.

4. Create workspaces
terraform workspace new dev
and:
terraform workspace new prod

Now the important thing is that Terraform keeps separate state for each workspace in the S3 backend.

Conceptually:
S3 bucket
│
├── dev
│    └── terraform.tfstate
│
└── prod
     └── terraform.tfstate

You don't manually create these state files. Terraform manages them.

5. Dev
terraform workspace select dev
terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"

Terraform stores the Dev state remotely.

6. Prod
terraform workspace select prod
terraform apply -var-file="prod.tfvars"

Terraform stores the Prod state separately.

So the complete flow is:

                 S3 BUCKET
                     │
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          ↓                     ↓
      DEV WORKSPACE          PROD WORKSPACE
          ↓                     ↓
      Dev state              Prod state
          ↓                     ↓
       Dev EC2                Prod EC2

You configure the S3 backend once in backend.tf; Terraform handles the workspace-specific state separation.

-------------------------------------------
In this configuration:

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
    key    = "terraform.tfstate"
    region = "ap-south-1"
  }
}

key = "terraform.tfstate" is the base state path.

With workspaces
Terraform automatically separates the workspace states.

Conceptually in S3:
my-terraform-state-bucket/
│
├── terraform.tfstate              ← default workspace
│
└── env:/
    ├── dev/
    │   └── terraform.tfstate      ← dev workspace
    │
    └── prod/
        └── terraform.tfstate      ← prod workspace

So you do not configure a separate key for dev and prod.

You write only:   key = "terraform.tfstate"
Terraform automatically adds the workspace-specific path.

Think of it this way
key = "terraform.tfstate"
          ↓
   Base state location
          ↓
   ┌──────┼──────┐
   ↓      ↓      ↓
default  dev    prod
   ↓      ↓      ↓
 state   state   state

If you select:  terraform workspace select dev
Terraform uses the dev workspace state.

If you select:   terraform workspace select prod
Terraform uses the prod workspace state.

You don't manually create those workspace state files. Terraform creates/manages them automatically.

-----------------------------

If Terraform is running from an EC2 instance, that EC2 needs IAM permissions for two main things:
1. Permission to store Terraform state in S3

Terraform needs permission to read/write the state file:

S3 permissions:
├── s3:GetObject
├── s3:PutObject
├── s3:DeleteObject
└── s3:ListBucket

For example:
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": [
    "s3:GetObject",
    "s3:PutObject",
    "s3:DeleteObject"
  ],
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-terraform-state/*"
}

And:

{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "s3:ListBucket",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-terraform-state"
}


2. Permission to create the AWS infrastructure

This depends on what Terraform is creating.

For example, if Terraform creates:

EC2
VPC
Subnet
Internet Gateway
Route Table
Security Group
IAM Role

then the EC2's IAM role needs the corresponding AWS permissions, such as:

EC2      → ec2:RunInstances, ec2:TerminateInstances, ...
VPC      → ec2:CreateVpc, ec2:CreateSubnet, ...
IAM      → iam:CreateRole, iam:AttachRolePolicy, ...


Simple picture
User
  ↓
EC2
  ↓
Terraform
  ├──────────────→ S3
  │                 ↓
  │              terraform.tfstate
  │
  └──────────────→ AWS
                    ↓
              VPC / EC2 / SG / etc.

So the EC2 IAM role needs:

        EC2 IAM Role
             │
       ┌─────┴─────┐
       ↓           ↓
   S3 access    AWS resource
   (state)       access

Important: S3 permission only allows Terraform to manage the state file. It does not give permission to create EC2, VPC, etc. Those are separate permissions.


Case 1: EC2 has an IAM role
User
 ↓
EC2
 ↓
Terraform apply
 ↓
EC2 IAM Role
 ├── S3 permission
 └── EC2/VPC permissions
 ↓
AWS

When the user runs:

terraform apply

Terraform automatically gets credentials from the IAM role attached to that EC2.

So Terraform can:

S3 → store/update terraform.tfstate
EC2 → create EC2
VPC → create VPC
etc.
Case 2: EC2 has no required permission

Then Terraform tries to do something like:

terraform apply
      ↓
AWS
      ↓
Permission denied ❌

You need to attach an appropriate IAM role to the EC2 with the required permissions.



Important

The permissions must cover both:

S3 permissions
     ↓
Store/read Terraform state

AWS resource permissions
     ↓
Create/manage EC2, VPC, etc.

So your understanding is essentially:

User runs Terraform → Terraform uses the EC2's IAM role → AWS checks the role's permissions → if allowed, Terraform performs the operation.



If Terraform is running on an EC2

Then the important identity is normally the EC2 IAM role:

User
 ↓
EC2
 ↓
Terraform
 ↓
EC2 IAM Role
 ↓
AWS

So even if the human user has permission, that does not automatically give permissions to Terraform running on the EC2.




If the EC2 has no IAM role and there are no AWS credentials configured on the EC2, Terraform cannot authenticate.

Developer has permission ✅

       BUT

EC2 has no credentials ❌

       ↓

terraform apply

       ↓

❌ Access denied / credentials error
If EC2 has an IAM role
Developer
   ↓
  EC2
   ↓
Terraform
   ↓
EC2 IAM Role
   ↓
AWS
   ↓
✅ Works
The important point

Logging into an EC2 does NOT transfer your IAM permissions to that EC2.

Your permissions work only when Terraform is using your AWS credentials.

The EC2 role is the normal way to give Terraform permissions when Terraform runs on EC2.
